venus 0130: the sweetfilepatch alias "mad" expands to /usr/libexec/mobileactivationd

It expanded to /private/usr/libexec/mobileactivationd, which does not exist on the HFS root
(/usr is a real directory; /private only holds var/): every "mad:" patch logged "NOT PATCHED (err 2)"
on current kernels, while older kernels found the file.  Only the alias target changes: the patch
bytes, offsets and the argument format are untouched (OpenCode draft, reviewed by Claude).

diff --git a/bsd/dev/sweet_file_patch.c b/bsd/dev/sweet_file_patch.c
index dc96e2f..b350225 100644
--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_file_patch.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_file_patch.c
@@ -17,7 +17,7 @@
  * not contain them we log a mismatch and leave the file alone, so a
  * different build of the same binary is never corrupted.
  *
- * The path alias "mad" expands to /private/usr/libexec/mobileactivationd
+ * The path alias "mad" expands to /usr/libexec/mobileactivationd
  * to keep the kernel command line short.
  *
  * The patch thread starts with the root device driver, long before the
@@ -66,7 +66,7 @@ static void
 sfp_expand(const char *path, char *out, int outlen)
 {
 	if (strcmp(path, "mad") == 0) {
-		strlcpy(out, "/private/usr/libexec/mobileactivationd", outlen);
+		strlcpy(out, "/usr/libexec/mobileactivationd", outlen);
 	} else {
 		strlcpy(out, path, outlen);
 	}
